8. LAUNCHER LIFECYCLE AND OWNERSHIP FIXES
======================================================================

- Validate `SESSION` and `RUN_STAMP` as safe basenames.
- Reserve the main and provenance rendezvous ports atomically on the master.
  Reservations carry version, token, session, port, and creation time, and are
  released only by the owning token on launch failure, stop, or rank-zero exit.
- Prepublish a version-2 launch intent on every node before starting any tmux
  session. Bind active state, tmux metadata, and the log header to snapshot,
  log directory, target iteration, launch token, command SHA256, and epoch.
- Status now distinguishes startup grace, running, finalizing, completed,
  stopping/stopped, and rollback states. A completion marker is authoritative
  only when it matches the active target and the exact tmux session has exited.
  Stale markers and logs from older launches are rejected.
- The launcher forwards and validates hierarchical reduce, CPU leader, and
  rank-visible switches. Hierarchical Gloo launches still verify and preload
  the pinned NCCL library used by local groups.
- During the first formal launch attempt, controlled stop exposed a real FD
  inheritance deadlock: the launcher held flock FD8 while calling
  `tmux new-session`; tmux and its long-running pane inherited FD8, so stop
  blocked waiting for the job to release the lock that stop needed to kill it.
- Fixed the deadlock by invoking `tmux new-session ... 8>&-`. The launcher
  parent retains the lock through tmux option and active-state binding, so the
  fix closes the FD only in the persistent child and does not introduce a
  check/create race.
- Added a dynamic regression assertion in the fake tmux process using
  `/proc/$$/fd/8`; the old implementation exits with an error and the fixed
  implementation sees FD8 closed. Dry-run contracts also require the explicit
  `8>&-` suffix.

