14. POST-SNAPSHOT RESUME/POLICY-INIT FAIL-CLOSED HARDENING
======================================================================

A separate workspace follow-up closed the low-priority checkpoint validation
gaps identified above. This change is deliberately *not* part of the immutable
r21 source snapshot and does not justify restarting the healthy fresh r21 job;
it applies to future snapshots and resume/policy-init operations.

The shared checkpoint validator now rejects missing, boolean, fractional,
negative, or contradictory completed/next iteration counters. Legacy files may
omit `next_iter`, but an explicit value must equal `iter + 1`. Model,
normalizer, and optimizer trees are recursively checked for non-finite numeric
values. Every optimizer parameter-group LR must be a finite positive real
inside the independently configured actor or critic bounds; the first actor
and critic group rates are restored separately.

Full PPO resume performs these checks for actor, critic, enabled normalizers,
and both optimizer states before mutating any live model, normalizer,
optimizer, iteration, fixed-BC, or environment state. Actor-only policy init
checks the actor and, when enabled, the actor normalizer before mutation; the
unused critic is intentionally ignored. The launcher preflights and direct PPO
load paths share the same numerical validators, so an alternate caller cannot
silently load non-finite numerical state.

Regression covering policy-init/resume preflight, direct PPO loads, schedule,
distillation, and fixed-BC semantics passed 193 tests. The fixed
`model_39999.pt` policy initializer also passed the new preflight. Python
compilation and diff/whitespace checks passed; `ruff` was unavailable in the
active environment.

