17. PURE-RL PROVENANCE, CONTACT-ID, AND STUDENT INFERENCE CLOSURE
======================================================================

The object-generalist launcher previously exported no
`HOLOSOMA_TRAINING_PROVENANCE`; runtime finalization, data/source revalidation,
cross-rank equality, checkpoint lineage, and checkpoint embedding therefore
all became no-ops. This was a real scientific-resume gap.

- Provenance v2 now explicitly distinguishes `distillation` and `pure_rl` and
  records `teacher_enabled`. Pure RL uses a domain-separated disabled-teacher
  digest rather than inventing a teacher artifact. Legacy v2 payloads normalize
  only to distillation, preserving old distill checkpoint compatibility.
- The generalist generator content-hashes motion/map/transitive objects,
  contact, source, policy-init, and training-resume inputs. Checkpoint
  references are first resolved to exact local immutable files. Source is
  recomputed on every pre-simulator helper invocation even when the large-data
  identity cache is reusable.
- The launcher binds adaptive contact roots to the hashed contact tree,
  rejects teacher-rollout reference rewards until those assets receive their
  own manifest, rejects `command:*`/`reward:*` selectors, and canonicalizes
  underscore/hyphen spellings before protecting provenance-owned CLI fields.
- NCCL execution requires the pinned library path and SHA256. Old generalist
  checkpoints with no saved provenance intentionally cannot be scientific
  resumes; they may only be treated as explicitly validated policy initializers.

Contact directory matching was also corrected. Splitting every name at the
first underscore corrupted exact IDs such as `box_10`. The parser now removes
only a nonempty decimal exporter prefix. When active motion IDs are available,
an exact active name wins before prefix interpretation, so a real ID such as
`2024_box_10` remains intact. Exact and numbered directories resolving to the
same active clip are rejected. Invalid metadata for an active clip fails
closed in adaptive sampling, offline guidance, rollout reward, validation, and
split inference. Shell-side launch validators use the same priority.

Student inference contracts now explicitly cover current split/history-1
175-D, object-velocity-v2/history-1 181-D, and current/legacy history-5 875-D
observations with exact aliases and eval selection. Split MuJoCo uses simulator
time, finite-value checks, and the timestamped shared-memory protocol; readers
select an observation at or before the requested simulation timestamp and may
not consume a future perception frame.

