======================================================================
22. END-TO-END NON-FINITE FAIL-CLOSED TRAINING BOUNDARIES
======================================================================

The numerical audit found several real masking paths: actor mean/std could be
`nan_to_num`-projected, teacher `clamp` could turn an infinity into a finite
target, non-finite logging values could be recorded as zero, only the two
headline losses were checked, gradient failures could skip an update, and a
checkpoint could be published with corrupt optimizer or buffer state.

The corrected contract is:

- Actor loc is never sanitized.  Std/scale projections modify finite entries
  only and preserve NaN/Inf.  Actor optimizer steps validate raw std before
  applying finite domain constraints.
- Every rollout step collapses raw/normalized actor and critic observations,
  all active student/teacher perception tensors, actor action/mean/std/log
  probability, critic value, multi-teacher selector, teacher action, and the
  exact `actions_to_step` into one device-side two-bit
  `[runtime_error, nonfinite]` verdict.  One all-rank MAX occurs before
  `env.step`; no tensor-by-tensor host synchronization occurs on success.
- Actor/critic forward, missing perception, teacher observation construction,
  multi-teacher selector/forward, fixed-BC capture/mixing, and log-probability
  exceptions reach that same verdict.  A bad rank and all healthy peers stop
  before PhysX sees the transition.
- The completed floating rollout storage, including rewards, returns and
  advantages, is checked before minibatch generation.  Teacher clipping only
  changes finite entries.
- Every numerical PPO loss field is checked before backward.  Non-finite loss
  or gradient verdicts raise on every rank; no update is skipped and no value
  is logged as zero.  `max_grad_norm` must be finite and positive, and actor
  and critic clipping use `error_if_nonfinite=True`.
- Ordinary backward, streamed supervised microbatch forward/loss/backward,
  gradient clipping, optimizer step, and std projection use synchronized
  rank-error envelopes.  At the iteration boundary actor/critic parameters,
  floating buffers (including BN running state), and floating optimizer
  tensors/scalars are checked.
- Before checkpoint publication, actor/critic state (including buffers), std
  constraints, both optimizer schemas/LRs/states, enabled normalizers, and the
  final collected payload must all be finite and compatible.  Any rank-local
  validation error prevents publication.

