28. PARTIAL-RESET PERCEPTION AND TEMPORAL-NOISE CORRECTNESS
======================================================================

The environment reset callback now carries the exact reset `env_ids` into
perception, and shared actor/critic/teacher manager aliases are deduplicated by
object identity.  WBT and locomotion therefore refresh only the reset subset;
surviving environments keep their camera observations and latency buffers.
A targeted reset is not a second control-frame sensor tick, so it also leaves
the global camera frequency phase unchanged.

A second independent review found that the first partial-reset implementation
normalized the full-update slice into an env-id tensor before preprocessing.
The Perlin hole generator consequently classified every ordinary full camera
refresh as partial and never advanced `frame_idx`.  Full sensor refreshes now
explicitly advance the temporal field once, whereas a reset-subset refresh
reuses the most recent field without advancing it.

The same review found that normalizing a subset Perlin frame by subset extrema
changes its hole mask relative to the identical environment in the preceding
full tick.  Full-tick extrema are now cached as derived state and reused for a
partial refresh.  The cache is deliberately cleared at canonical reset and
checkpoint load: if a resume first performs a partial refresh, the full-batch
extrema are deterministically rebuilt from authenticated `frame_idx - 1`
(`frame_idx == 0` uses frame zero) without consuming the temporal clock.
Regression tests prove the partial mask is bit-identical to the selected full
mask, including cache loss and the frame-zero boundary.  Latency, additive,
edge and depth-buffer writes remain restricted to reset environments.

The combined partial-reset, perception-manager and WBT checkpoint suite passed
99 tests.  Python compilation and `git diff --check` also passed.

