32. LAUNCHER EXACT-IDENTITY, SHARED-LOG AND CONTROL-PROTOCOL CLOSURE
======================================================================

The final launcher review found that both `/proc` identity scanners inherited
the status of the last comparison executed in their `while read` body.  A
same-token process whose last environment field contained a wrong epoch could
therefore make the loop return one and be misclassified as unrelated.  Each
body now terminates with an unconditional success sentinel; wrong, missing,
duplicate or malformed token/command/epoch fields reach the exact post-loop
validation and fail closed.  Real `/proc` fixtures cover terminal status and
predecessor cleanup for wrong/missing epochs.  Fixture processes now remain
alive for the whole test and are removed only by exact PID+token cleanup.

Generated startup and stop commands had two shell-expansion defects.  Startup
health referenced an unbound remote `EXPORT_ONNX` instead of the immutable
controller value.  The stop heredoc also let `$#` in a sed end-of-line pattern
expand to the controller's positional count, producing an invalid expression.
The expected export mode is now explicitly embedded, and the sed anchor is
escaped.  Manual launch, two readiness rounds and exact stop/process closure
were exercised in addition to the contract suite.

Training completion is a whitespace-delimited control record, so final
`LOGGER_BASE_DIR` and `TRAINING_NAME` values now reject whitespace/control
characters before snapshot or remote mutation.  `TRAINING_NAME` is also a
bounded safe basename and cannot contain slash or traversal.  Resume and local
checkpoint helpers share one persisted actor validator for canonical MLP,
MLP-perception, Flow and Flow-perception types, ordered unique inputs, hidden
dims and Flow parameters.  This removes launcher-only reinterpretations of the
same checkpoint schema.

The first fresh-log hardening required every node to create the entire
run-specific log directory.  That is invalid when `RUN_REPO` is shared/NFS:
rank zero creates it and rank one necessarily sees a collision.  The launcher
now creates a per-publisher token/node/PID staging directory, writes a mode
0400 one-line/eight-field owner record
`(version,snapshot,session,log,target,nnodes,token,epoch)`, validates it, and
atomically publishes the directory with `mv -T --no-clobber`.  A losing node
may share the destination only when all eight fields match; it removes only
its own exact staging path.  Missing, extra-field, wrong-target, foreign or
symlinked owners remain untouched and fail closed.  Per-node log files still
use no-clobber creation.  A two-node shared-filesystem fixture proves both
logs coexist, subset/reordered stop is rejected, and full ordered stop closes
both sessions and releases the rendezvous pair.

The two-node partial-rollback watchdog was 14 seconds while the deliberately
injected startup, lifecycle transition, rollback and final-closure sequence
can legitimately take about 14.1-14.5 seconds under its unchanged internal
two-second bounds.  Only the test's outer watchdog is now 30 seconds; the
production startup, probe, cleanup and lock bounds remain unchanged.  The
fixture proves rank zero reaches exact process/session closure, the hanging
rank remains `rolling_back`, and both ports stay quarantined.

