35. FIXED-BC FORGETTING GUARD AND R22 SCIENTIFIC HANDOFF
======================================================================

The old r21 fixed-set series exposed a scientific failure mode that ordinary
finite-loss and reward health checks cannot detect.  Its deterministic
mean-action MSE reached about 0.0803 during pure BC, then rose through 0.0878,
0.0868, 0.1037, 0.1145, 0.1249, 0.1380, 0.1496, 0.1524 and 0.1626 at the
successive 0.1 PPO tiers.  It was 0.2943 at iteration 10600 and 0.2982 at
iteration 10700 even though rewards, gradients, optimizer state and every
checkpoint tensor remained finite.  This makes r21 useful diagnostic evidence
but not an acceptable final scientific result.

The corrected r22 schedule therefore keeps a real pure-BC phase and increases
PPO by 0.1 every 700 iterations only through 0.7 at iteration 4900, retaining
0.3 BC thereafter.  The 0.7 choice is evidence-based: the old fixed-set value
at that tier remained below the 0.160 absolute ceiling, while later tiers
approached or crossed it.  This is a new fresh-run contract, not a resume or a
reinterpretation of r21's 0.9/6300 schedule.

A fail-closed fixed-BC guard now authenticates an exact global 4096-sample
dataset distributed by quotient/remainder over all 104 ranks.  The dataset is
digested locally and globally at its first evaluation and can never be silently
re-based.  The guard requires at least three aligned evaluations in a pure-BC
reference window, freezes
`min(reference_min * max_reference_ratio, absolute_max_mu_mse)`, and starts its
consecutive-exceedance counter only after the configured PPO ramp completes.
The formal contract uses reference end 600, ratio 2.0, absolute ceiling 0.160,
start 4900, interval 100 and patience 3.

Every guard transition validates the complete prior state, exact evaluation
timeline, weighted sample count, rank strata, allocation, dataset digests and
configuration fingerprint.  Metric, timeline, digest, transition, checkpoint,
RNG and logging failures are synchronized before any rank enters the next
collective.  Fixed evaluation and guard bookkeeping capture and restore
Python, NumPy, torch CPU and CUDA RNG state, so the diagnostic is
observational.  Normal checkpoints must contain complete non-tripped,
resumable state.  A tripped run first atomically publishes a diagnostic
checkpoint and marker whose state is explicitly non-resumable, then attempts
terminal W&B logging, and finally makes every rank exit in the same protocol.

This guard is intentionally described narrowly.  Because each rank fills its
small local quota from the first valid rollout batch, the current 4096 samples
are primarily a deterministic reset-state forgetting sentinel.  They do not
claim full on-policy occupancy coverage; motion/reward/contact metrics and the
real multi-node canary remain separate acceptance evidence.

W&B startup was also made an all-rank contract.  Rank zero must create the
expected cloud entity/project/run, and its exact identity or failure is
broadcast before any worker constructs the simulator.  Ambient run/project/id
aliases are cleared while credentials are preserved.  Epoch logging and
terminal guard logging now return an all-rank outcome, preventing rank-zero I/O
failure from stranding peers at checkpoint barriers.  The reference minimum is
summarized as `min`; the frozen threshold, counter and last MSE use `last`.

The final legacy-process review changed `/proc` environment reads to binary-safe
current-UID scans and rechecks UID, PPID and process start time immediately
before every signal.  Missing, duplicate, malformed or mismatched launch
identity remains fail-closed.  Launcher-side guard numeric checks now mirror
the Python/CLI checks, including finite real values, interval alignment,
pure-BC reference semantics and enough remaining evaluations to reach the
configured patience before the run target.

After these changes, the project-owned Python range ran 89 test files and
finished with 2209 passed, four expected skips and zero failures.  This scope
includes the IsaacSim state-accessor unit tests and excludes only vendored
packages, archived inference clones and the external IsaacGym/GPU-simulator
